      "dom_id": "step-q140636",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=13$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=13.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=169.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-169I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-169I=[\\begin{aligned}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{aligned}]-169[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-169I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{3}{5}|+|\\frac{4}{5}|=\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$",
