      "source_example_id": "e-8414",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition",
      "kp_id": "e-8414"
    },
    "q-96309": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96309",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=6.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-96309-a",
